// ── HAIR · learn ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const HAIR_LEARN = [
  { id: "postpartum-shedding", ic: "🤱", title: "Why postpartum shedding happens",
    desc: "It peaks around month four, and it is not hair loss in the way it feels.",
    body: ["Hair grows in cycles. At any moment most of your hair is in the growing phase and a smaller share is resting, waiting to fall. Pregnancy hormones hold far more hair than usual in the growing phase — which is why hair often looks its thickest at eight months pregnant.",
      "After birth those hormones drop, and all the hair that should have shed gradually over nine months exits at once. It usually starts around month two or three, peaks near month four, and settles by nine to twelve months.",
      "It is called telogen effluvium, it is temporary, and there is no product that stops it. What you can do is stop adding damage: gentle styles, satin at night, no tight ponytails on hair that is already letting go."],
    note: "If shedding is still heavy past twelve months, or you're also exhausted and cold, ask for ferritin and thyroid labs. Postpartum iron deficiency and thyroid changes are common and both show up in your hair." },
  { id: "protein-hair", ic: "🥚", title: "Protein and hair growth",
    desc: "Hair is mostly protein. Your body treats it as the least important place to spend it.",
    body: ["Hair is roughly 95% keratin, a protein. When protein intake is low, the body prioritises organs and muscle — hair is the first thing it stops funding and the last to be restored.",
      "This is why crash diets, poor postpartum eating and heavy restriction so often show up as thinning three months later. The delay is what makes it hard to connect.",
      "There is no evidence that eating extra protein beyond your needs grows hair faster. Meeting your needs consistently is what matters."],
    note: "If your hair is thinning and you're under-eating, no serum will outperform a proper breakfast." },
  { id: "breakage-vs-shedding", ic: "🔍", title: "Breakage vs shedding",
    desc: "They look the same in the shower and need opposite solutions.",
    body: ["Shed hair has a small white bulb at one end — the root. It completed its cycle and left naturally. Normal is 50–100 a day, more when you've skipped a wash.",
      "Broken hair has no bulb. It's short, often uneven, and snapped mid-shaft. That's mechanical or chemical damage, not a hair cycle problem.",
      "Check the ends of what's in your brush. Bulbs mean look at health, hormones and stress. No bulbs mean look at heat, brushing, elastics and colour."],
    note: "Treating breakage with growth supplements is the most common wasted purchase in hair care. Find out which one you have first." },
  { id: "hard-water", ic: "🚿", title: "Hard water and hair",
    desc: "If your hair changed when you moved house, this is probably why.",
    body: ["Hard water carries dissolved calcium and magnesium. Those minerals deposit on the hair shaft and build up over weeks, leaving hair dull, stiff, harder to lather and oddly resistant to conditioner.",
      "On colour-treated hair it also shifts tone, usually towards brassiness, and makes colour fade faster.",
      "A chelating or hard-water shampoo used every couple of weeks removes the deposits. A shower filter reduces how fast they return."],
    note: "If your hair feels coated no matter what you use, the problem may be your water, not your products." },
  { id: "scalp-health", ic: "🌱", title: "Scalp health",
    desc: "Hair grows out of skin, and skin has needs.",
    body: ["The scalp is skin with a very high density of follicles and oil glands. Like facial skin, it can be dry, oily, congested, inflamed or sensitised — and all of those affect what grows out of it.",
      "Flaking isn't always dryness. Persistent, greasy, yellowish flakes are usually seborrhoeic dermatitis, which responds to antifungal shampoo rather than more oil. Dry, small, white flakes on a tight scalp are usually genuine dryness.",
      "Treating the wrong one makes it worse, which is why so many people bounce between products for years."],
    note: "If your scalp is itchy, sore or flaking persistently, that's worth a GP or dermatologist visit rather than a fourth shampoo." },
  { id: "growth-myths", ic: "🚫", title: "Hair growth myths",
    desc: "What doesn't work, so you stop paying for it.",
    body: ["Trimming does not make hair grow faster. Growth happens at the follicle; the ends have no say. Trims preserve length by removing splits before they travel.",
      "Most 'growth' shampoos cannot work as claimed — they're on your scalp for under a minute and rinsed away. Leave-on scalp treatments at least have contact time.",
      "Biotin supplements only help if you're deficient, which is rare. In everyone else they do nothing for hair and can skew thyroid and troponin blood tests, which matters more than people realise.",
      "Hair grows roughly 1cm a month. Nothing sold over the counter meaningfully changes that number."],
    note: "Tell any clinician you take biotin before blood tests — it can distort thyroid and cardiac results badly enough to cause real confusion." },
]

