const MAKEUP_LEARN = [
  { id: "undertone", ic: "🎨", title: "Finding your undertone",
    desc: "Cool, warm or neutral — and why it matters more than depth.",
    body: ["Undertone is the colour beneath your skin's surface, and it doesn't change with a tan. Cool undertones lean pink, red or blue; warm lean yellow, peach or gold; neutral sits between.",
      "Two quick checks: does silver or gold jewellery look better on you, and do the veins at your inner wrist read blue-purple or green? Silver and blue-purple suggest cool; gold and green suggest warm. If neither is obvious, you're probably neutral.",
      "Matching depth wrong makes foundation look too light or dark. Matching undertone wrong makes it look grey or orange, which is far more noticeable."],
    note: "Test foundation on your jawline in daylight and leave it ten minutes. Most formulas oxidise slightly and darken." },
  { id: "foundation-match", ic: "🧴", title: "Foundation matching",
    desc: "Why the shade that looked right in store looks wrong at home.",
    body: ["Store lighting is designed to flatter, and it is almost never the light you'll actually be seen in. Swatch on the jaw, then look in daylight.",
      "Skin changes depth by season, sometimes by two shades. Many people need a summer and a winter shade, or one shade they mix.",
      "Oxidation is real — most foundations shift slightly darker or more orange as they interact with skin oils. Wait ten minutes before judging."],
    note: "If you're between two shades, take the lighter one and warm it with bronzer. Too dark is much harder to fix than too light." },
  { id: "cream-vs-powder", ic: "🧈", title: "Cream vs powder",
    desc: "The single biggest factor in whether makeup looks natural on you.",
    body: ["Cream and liquid formulas contain emollients, so they melt into skin and move with it. Powder sits on top and absorbs oil.",
      "On dry, mature or textured skin, powder settles into lines and emphasises them. Cream does the opposite.",
      "On very oily skin, powder genuinely helps — but usually only in the T-zone, not across the whole face.",
      "As a rule: cream first, powder only where you truly need it."],
    note: "If your makeup looks worse at hour four than hour one, you're probably using powder where cream would have worn better." },
  { id: "mascara-transfer", ic: "👁️", title: "Why mascara transfers",
    desc: "It's usually not the mascara.",
    body: ["Transfer under the brow bone or on the lid is usually contact — hooded or deep-set eyes touch the lashes when you blink. Waterproof or tubing formulas solve it.",
      "Transfer under the eye is more often oil, from skincare, eye cream or your own skin, breaking the formula down.",
      "Tubing mascaras wrap each lash in a polymer rather than coating it in pigment. They don't smudge and remove with warm water alone."],
    note: "If you have oily lids and use rich eye cream, keep the cream to the orbital bone rather than up under the brow." },
  { id: "makeup-expiry", ic: "📅", title: "Makeup expiration",
    desc: "What genuinely needs replacing, and what doesn't.",
    body: ["Mascara and liquid liner: three months. The wand returns bacteria to a warm wet tube every use, and eye infections are the most common makeup-related problem there is.",
      "Liquid foundation and concealer: about a year. Cream products: 12–18 months. Powders and pressed products: two years or more, since bacteria struggle without water.",
      "Lipstick: about two years. Discard anything that smells different, separates or changes texture, whatever the date says."],
    note: "Never share eye makeup, and never use it while you have any eye infection — throw that mascara out when the infection clears." },
  { id: "sanitizing", ic: "🧼", title: "Sanitizing makeup",
    desc: "The part almost nobody does, and the one a nurse would insist on.",
    body: ["Brushes used with cream or liquid products should be washed weekly; powder brushes every two to three weeks. Gentle shampoo, lukewarm water, dry flat with bristles off the edge of a surface.",
      "Never dry brushes upright — water runs into the ferrule, dissolves the glue and rots the handle.",
      "Sponges are the worst offender: damp, porous and usually stored in a warm bathroom. Wash after every use and replace every one to three months.",
      "Sharpen pencils before each use to expose a fresh surface."],
    note: "Breakouts along the jaw and cheeks that resist skincare are sometimes just a dirty foundation brush." },
  { id: "color-theory", ic: "🌈", title: "Color theory",
    desc: "Why colour correcting works, in one paragraph.",
    body: ["Colours opposite each other on the wheel cancel each other out. That's the whole principle behind colour correcting.",
      "Green cancels red, so it neutralises rosacea and blemishes. Peach and orange cancel blue, so they counteract dark circles — peach for lighter skin, deeper orange for deeper skin. Lavender counters yellow dullness.",
      "Use the smallest amount that works and apply it before concealer, not after. Too much correcter reads grey."],
    note: "For most people, a peach-toned concealer under the eye is all the colour correcting they'll ever need." },
]


