const BROW_LEARN = [
  { id: "brow-blindness", ic: "👀", title: "What is eyebrow blindness?",
    desc: "You're not going mad. You just stopped seeing them.",
    body: ["Eyebrow blindness is when you're so used to seeing your own brows that you stop noticing how they actually frame your face. Trends change, faces change, and what once looked balanced can slowly become too dark, too thin, too boxy or too high without you realising it.",
      "It happens because you see your brows every single day. There's no before-and-after moment, just a hundred tiny adjustments in the same direction — a little darker each month, a little more product each morning, a few more hairs off each time you tidy them. Your brain updates its idea of normal along the way, so the mirror always looks fine.",
      "The 2010s were a mass demonstration of this. An entire decade filled in dramatically dark, sharply squared brows and genuinely could not see it — until the photographs came back a few years later. The same thing happened with the pencil-thin brows of the 90s, and it will happen again with whatever we're all doing now.",
      "The tells are recognisable once you look for them: brows noticeably darker than your hair, a front edge with a hard vertical line, an arch that has crept toward the centre of the eye, a tail that stops short, or the fact that you now need a specific product every day for your brows to look 'right'.",
      "Two things fix it. Brow mapping gives you fixed reference points that don't drift, because your nostril and your eye corner don't move even when your habits do. And asking someone you trust — someone who won't just be polite — for a genuinely fresh opinion. Photographs help too; a camera hasn't been looking at your face every day.",
      "The goal isn't perfect brows. It's brows that suit your face."],
    note: "Brows are one of the first things your eyes notice on a face. Tiny adjustments often make a bigger difference than buying an expensive new makeup product." },
  { id: "nano-vs-micro", ic: "⚖️", title: "Nano Brows vs Microblading",
    desc: "The comparison worth reading before you book either.",
    body: ["Both are semi-permanent pigment placed into the upper layers of skin to create the look of individual brow hairs. The difference is the instrument, and it changes almost everything downstream.",
      "Microblading uses a hand-held tool with a row of very fine blades that cut fine channels into the skin, and pigment settles into those cuts. Nano brows — sometimes called nano-blading or machine hair strokes — use a digital device with a single ultra-fine needle that deposits pigment point by point, rather than slicing.",
      "SKIN TYPE. This is the deciding factor for most people. Microblading heals most predictably on dry to normal skin. On oily skin, larger pores and higher sebum cause the fine strokes to blur and spread as they heal, so crisp hair strokes can turn into a soft shadow within a year. Nano needling causes less trauma and deposits more precisely, so it holds its definition considerably better on oily and combination skin. It's also generally the better choice for mature or thin skin, where blades can cause more bruising.",
      "LONGEVITY. Microblading typically lasts one to two years before fading. Nano brows usually last two to three, partly because pigment is deposited more consistently at the right depth. Both need a top-up appointment six to eight weeks after the first session, and both will fade — that's by design, not a fault.",
      "HEALING. Microblading creates small cuts, so healing involves more visible scabbing and roughly ten to fourteen days of looking considerably darker than the final result. Nano brows tend to heal faster with less scabbing, though they still darken dramatically before settling. With either, the colour at day three is not your colour — it typically lightens by 30–50% as it heals.",
      "APPEARANCE. Nano brows generally produce a softer, more natural result. Because each stroke is built from many tiny pigment points rather than one continuous cut, the strokes can be varied in density and thickness the way real brow hair varies. They blend more convincingly with existing hair, and they tend to age more gracefully — fading into a soft shadow rather than into blurred lines.",
      "The honest summary: nano brows are more forgiving across more skin types, last longer, and look softer. Microblading remains lovely on the right skin with the right artist. In both cases the artist matters more than the technique."],
    note: "Ask any artist for healed photos at twelve months, not fresh work. Anyone can make brows look good on day one — healed results are what you're actually buying. And neither is done during pregnancy or breastfeeding." },
  { id: "brows-face", ic: "🖼️", title: "How eyebrows change your face",
    desc: "More than any other feature you can adjust in five minutes.",
    body: ["Brows frame the eyes and set the visual weight of the upper face. Small changes in position read as large changes in expression, which is why the same face can look tired, severe or surprised depending purely on brow shape.",
      "A higher arch opens the eye and reads as alert; too high reads as startled. A brow that tapers below its starting point drags the outer eye down and reads as tired, which is why extending a short tail is one of the fastest lifts available without a needle.",
      "Thickness carries age signalling too. Fuller brows generally read younger, which is part of why the fashion swung so hard away from the thin brows of the 90s.",
      "Depth matters as much as shape. Brows more than a shade or two darker than your hair pull focus and harden the whole face."],
    note: "If you want to look less tired, extending the brow tail slightly is usually more effective than any concealer under your eyes." },
  { id: "grow-brows", ic: "🌱", title: "Growing brows naturally",
    desc: "Slow, unglamorous, and mostly about leaving them alone.",
    body: ["Brow hairs have a growth cycle of roughly four months, far shorter than scalp hair. That's why brows can't grow long, and why regrowth takes a season rather than weeks.",
      "The main intervention is genuinely doing nothing: no plucking at all for three to four months. Most people cannot manage this, which is the actual reason their brows never fill in.",
      "Repeated plucking over years can damage the follicle permanently. Areas over-plucked in your twenties may simply not return in your forties.",
      "Peptide serums have modest evidence and work only while used. Prostaglandin-analogue serums work better but can cause redness, darkening of the skin and, rarely, changes in iris colour if they migrate to the eye."],
    note: "Castor oil has no evidence for brow growth. It's a decent conditioner that makes existing hairs look glossier and thicker, which is why the before-and-afters look convincing." },
  { id: "brow-myths", ic: "🚫", title: "Brow myths",
    desc: "Four things that aren't true.",
    body: ["'Shaving makes hair grow back thicker.' It doesn't. Cutting a hair at the surface leaves a blunt end that feels coarser, but nothing about the follicle changes.",
      "'Your brows should match exactly.' They shouldn't, and they don't on anyone. Forcing symmetry usually means over-plucking one side to match a mistake on the other.",
      "'Everyone should have a high arch.' Arch height should follow your own bone structure and hair growth. A flat brow on the right face is elegant; a high arch on the wrong one is permanently surprised.",
      "'Vaseline grows brows.' It's occlusive — it holds moisture in and makes hairs lie neatly. It cannot reach the follicle."],
    note: "If your brows have thinned noticeably at the outer third and you're also tired, cold or gaining weight, that's worth a thyroid check. It's a genuinely classic sign." },
]


